GOAL: Fix a header-injection risk in `c.redirect` by percent-encoding raw CR (`\r` -> `%0D`) and LF (`\n` -> `%0A`) in the `Location` header, while preserving existing valid-redirect behavior (plain URLs unchanged, multibyte URLs encoded exactly as before, already-percent-encoded inputs NOT double-encoded).

Only edit these two files:
1. src/context.ts
2. src/context.test.ts

Do NOT edit any other files. Do NOT run git add / git commit / git stage. Do NOT run formatters that rewrite files (you may run read-only `--check` commands). Do not spawn subagents.

=== src/context.ts ===
Locate the `redirect` method (currently around lines 768-780). It currently reads:

  redirect = <T extends RedirectStatusCode = 302>(
    location: string | URL,
    status?: T
  ): Response & TypedResponse<undefined, T, 'redirect'> => {
    const locationString = String(location)
    this.header(
      'Location',
      // Multibytes should be encoded
      // eslint-disable-next-line no-control-regex
      !/[^\x00-\xFF]/.test(locationString) ? locationString : encodeURI(locationString)
    )
    return this.newResponse(null, status ?? 302) as any
  }

Replace it with this implementation (keep the exact formatting):

  redirect = <T extends RedirectStatusCode = 302>(
    location: string | URL,
    status?: T
  ): Response & TypedResponse<undefined, T, 'redirect'> => {
    const locationString = String(location)
    // Multibytes should be encoded, and CR / LF must be percent-encoded to
    // prevent header injection. `encodeURI()` already encodes CR / LF.
    /* eslint-disable no-control-regex */
    const encodedLocation = !/[^\x00-\xFF]/.test(locationString)
      ? locationString.replace(/[\r\n]/g, (char) => (char === '\r' ? '%0D' : '%0A'))
      : encodeURI(locationString)
    /* eslint-enable no-control-regex */
    this.header('Location', encodedLocation)
    return this.newResponse(null, status ?? 302) as any
  }

Rationale (do not change the logic): `encodeURI()` already encodes CR/LF, so the vulnerability exists only in the byte-string branch (no code unit > 0xFF). That branch now explicitly encodes CR/LF. The multibyte branch keeps the exact prior `encodeURI()` behavior.

=== src/context.test.ts ===
Add regression tests inside the top-level `describe('Context', ...)` block, immediately AFTER the existing `unchangedURLString.forEach(...)` block that ends around line 130 (right before `it('c.body() - Blob', ...)`). Add exactly these tests:

  it('c.redirect() encodes CR and LF in the Location header', () => {
    const res = c.redirect('/destination\r\nX-Injected: true')
    expect(res.status).toBe(302)
    expect(res.headers.get('Location')).toBe('/destination%0D%0AX-Injected: true')
    expect(res.headers.get('Location')).not.toMatch(/[\r\n]/)
    expect(res.headers.get('X-Injected')).toBeNull()
  })

  it('c.redirect() encodes a lone CR and a lone LF', () => {
    let res = c.redirect('/destination\rX-Injected: true')
    expect(res.headers.get('Location')).toBe('/destination%0DX-Injected: true')
    res = c.redirect('/destination\nX-Injected: true')
    expect(res.headers.get('Location')).toBe('/destination%0AX-Injected: true')
  })

  it('c.redirect() encodes CR and LF with multibyte characters', () => {
    const res = c.redirect('/こんにちは\r\nX-Injected: true')
    const location = res.headers.get('Location')
    expect(location).toContain('%0D%0A')
    expect(location).not.toMatch(/[\r\n]/)
    expect(res.headers.get('X-Injected')).toBeNull()
  })

  it('c.redirect() keeps percent-encoded CR and LF unchanged', () => {
    for (const location of [
      '/destination%0d%0aX-Injected:%20true',
      '/destination%0D%0ASet-Cookie:%20injected=true',
    ]) {
      const res = c.redirect(location)
      expect(res.status).toBe(302)
      expect(res.headers.get('Location')).toBe(location)
    }
  })

